{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01ChyFaPvLUGH6EVqZp8X9t8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RWfdTsJYXNw42sgCB6Azp8","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_7/projects/gen-depth-contamination/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260512T200328/median/paper.tex","limit":200},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1437,"cache_read_input_tokens":19009,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1437,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":60,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018paoR6xr2NTD6NpJCLtAVH","session_id":"23a92ae3-d412-49aa-a360-fb90b813cfb5","uuid":"a677899b-55fa-4007-8d41-9e2389986dbe"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RWfdTsJYXNw42sgCB6Azp8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n6\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n11\t\\usepackage{cleveref}\n12\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n13\t\n14\t\\title{How Deep Is Your Data? Measuring Generational Saturation Boundaries\\\\in Recursive Synthetic Text}\n15\t\n16\t\\author{Anonymous}\n17\t\n18\t\\begin{document}\n19\t\\maketitle\n20\t\n21\t\\begin{abstract}\n22\tWeb-scale corpora are increasingly contaminated by synthetic text that has been recursively generated---text produced by language models trained on or prompted with prior synthetic outputs. Current detection methods treat synthetic text as a binary category (human vs.\\ machine-generated), ignoring the progressive quality degradation across recursive generations. We formalize \\emph{generational depth estimation} as a prediction task and introduce the \\emph{saturation boundary} $K^*$, the maximum generation depth at which consecutive generations remain statistically distinguishable. Through controlled experiments across three model families (Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, Qwen2.5-1.5B), four decoding strategies, and two seed datasets, we establish $K^*=2$ as a remarkably stable empirical constant for 1--2B parameter models: the Jensen--Shannon divergence between feature distributions decays from 0.27 at the human/gen-1 boundary to below 0.005 by generation 3. Only extreme-temperature decoding ($T\\geq 1.2$) occasionally extends $K^*$ to 3. A cross-scale probe with Qwen2.5-7B provides suggestive evidence that $K^*$ may increase with model scale. We further show that even the coarse three-class signal (human / gen-1 / gen-2+) derived from $K^*=2$ enables \\emph{graduated filtering} that outperforms binary filtering by 0.8 percentage points on MMLU and 0.7 on HellaSwag in continued pretraining, with the advantage growing super-linearly as contamination increases. Our results provide the first systematic characterization of generational saturation in recursive synthetic text and actionable guidance for depth-aware data curation.\n23\t\\end{abstract}\n24\t\n25\t\\section{Introduction}\n26\t\n27\tThe fraction of machine-generated text on the web is growing rapidly. As language models (LMs) are trained on web-scraped data that already contains their predecessors' outputs, a recursive feedback loop emerges: each generation of models produces text that seeds the next generation's training data. This phenomenon---variously called \\emph{model collapse}~\\cite{shumailov2024ai}, \\emph{data contamination}, or \\emph{recursive synthetic pollution}---threatens the quality of future training corpora.\n28\t\n29\tExisting detection methods frame the problem as binary classification: is a given text human-written or machine-generated? Tools such as DivEye and Binoculars achieve strong performance on this binary task (AUC $>0.93$). However, they are fundamentally blind to \\emph{how many times} a text has passed through the generative pipeline. First-generation synthetic text (gen-1) retains much of the original distribution's richness, while fourth-generation text (gen-4) may exhibit severe vocabulary collapse, flattened surprisal dynamics, and factual drift. Treating both identically discards actionable quality information.\n30\t\n31\tWe make three contributions:\n32\t\n33\t\\paragraph{C1: Saturation Boundary ($K^*$).} We formalize the \\emph{generational discrimination boundary} $K^*$ as the maximum depth $k$ at which pairwise classification between generation $k$ and $k+1$ exceeds chance (AUC $>0.60$). From information-theoretic first principles---specifically, the Data Processing Inequality (DPI)---discriminability must decay monotonically along any Markov chain of generative processes. $K^*$ marks where this decay reaches a practical floor. Through systematic measurement across three model families, four decoding strategies, and base/instruct variants, we establish $K^*=2$ as a robust empirical constant for current-generation 1--2B parameter models. This is, to our knowledge, the first controlled measurement of this quantity.\n34\t\n35\t\\paragraph{C2: Linguistic Degradation Ordering.} We analyze which linguistic features saturate first during recursive generation. Vocabulary diversity consistently degrades earliest across all three model families, while the relative ordering of surprisal dynamics and tail-thinning features is model-dependent (Kendall $\\tau = 0.67$ across model pairs). We report this as a \\emph{tendency} rather than a strict ordering.\n36\t\n37\t\\paragraph{C3: Graduated Filtering.} We demonstrate that depth-graduated filtering---using the three-class signal human/gen-1/gen-2+ enabled by $K^*=2$---yields better-calibrated quality estimates than binary filtering. In continued pretraining experiments, graduated filtering improves over binary filtering by 0.8 pp on MMLU and 0.7 pp on HellaSwag at 30\\% contamination, with the advantage scaling to 1.1 pp at 50\\% contamination. The mechanism is improved calibration near the decision boundary, not a large accuracy gap.\n38\t\n39\t\\section{Related Work}\n40\t\n41\t\\paragraph{Binary Synthetic Text Detection.} A rich literature addresses distinguishing human from machine-generated text. Watermarking approaches embed detectable signals during generation. Post-hoc detectors exploit statistical signatures: DivEye~\\cite{diveye} uses divergence-based features, Binoculars~\\cite{binoculars} compares cross-model perplexities, and Min-K\\%++~\\cite{minkpp} thresholds on low-probability tokens. All operate in a binary paradigm and do not estimate generational depth.\n42\t\n43\t\\paragraph{Model Collapse and Recursive Training.} \\citet{shumailov2024ai} demonstrate that iteratively training on self-generated data leads to model collapse---progressive loss of distributional tails. Subsequent work characterizes collapse dynamics in specific settings. However, this line provides descriptive analysis of the collapse phenomenon rather than detection or curation tools.\n44\t\n45\t\\paragraph{Verification-Based Quality Filtering.} Recent approaches use external verifiers to gate training data quality. \\citet{feng2024} and \\citet{yi2024} employ LLM-as-judge or factual verification to filter low-quality synthetic data. These methods perform binary quality gating without estimating recursive depth, forgoing the ordinal structure that depth estimation provides.\n46\t\n47\t\\paragraph{Gap.} No existing method estimates how many rounds of recursive generation a text has undergone. We are the first to formalize depth estimation as a prediction task and to measure the empirical saturation boundary $K^*$.\n48\t\n49\t\\section{Problem Formulation}\n50\t\n51\t\\subsection{Recursive Generation as Iterated Lossy Channel}\n52\t\n53\tLet $P_0$ denote the distribution of human-authored text. A language model $M$ defines a stochastic map $\\mathcal{C}_M: \\mathcal{X} \\to \\mathcal{X}$ that takes an input text and produces a synthetic output. Recursive generation creates a Markov chain of distributions:\n54\t\\begin{equation}\n55\tP_0 \\xrightarrow{\\mathcal{C}_M} P_1 \\xrightarrow{\\mathcal{C}_M} P_2 \\xrightarrow{\\mathcal{C}_M} \\cdots\n56\t\\end{equation}\n57\tEach application of $\\mathcal{C}_M$ is a lossy channel: it preserves some statistical properties of its input while compressing others. By the Data Processing Inequality, for any divergence measure $D$:\n58\t\\begin{equation}\n59\tD(P_k \\| P_{k+1}) \\leq D(P_{k-1} \\| P_k) \\quad \\forall\\, k \\geq 1\n60\t\\end{equation}\n61\tThis monotonic decay guarantees the existence of a saturation boundary: there exists some $K^*$ beyond which consecutive generations are practically indistinguishable.\n62\t\n63\t\\subsection{Saturation Boundary $K^*$}\n64\t\n65\tWe operationalize $K^*$ using pairwise classification:\n66\t\\begin{equation}\n67\tK^* = \\max\\{k : \\text{AUC}(k, k+1) > \\theta\\}\n68\t\\end{equation}\n69\twhere $\\text{AUC}(k, k+1)$ is the area under the ROC curve for distinguishing generation $k$ from generation $k+1$ using a fixed feature set, and $\\theta = 0.60$ is a discrimination threshold. We validate robustness to $\\theta$ in \\Cref{sec:threshold_sensitivity}.\n70\t\n71\t\\subsection{Depth Estimation as Ordinal Prediction}\n72\t\n73\tGiven a text $x$, the depth estimation task is to predict its generational depth $d(x) \\in \\{0, 1, \\ldots, K\\}$. The ordinal nature of depth---$d=0$ is closer to $d=1$ than to $d=3$---motivates ordinal regression approaches over standard multinomial classification.\n74\t\n75\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n76\t\n77\t\\subsection{Depth Chain Generation}\n78\t\n79\tWe construct recursive generation chains (\\emph{depth chains}) using three model families at the 1--2B scale:\n80\t\\begin{itemize}\n81\t\\item \\textbf{Pythia-1.4B}: GPT-NeoX architecture, trained on The Pile (300B tokens).\n82\t\\item \\textbf{OLMo-1B}: Transformer++ architecture, trained on Dolma.\n83\t\\item \\textbf{Qwen2.5-1.5B}: Qwen-style architecture with multilingual pretraining.\n84\t\\end{itemize}\n85\t\n86\tFor each model, we sample 5,000 documents from C4 as seed texts ($P_0$) and generate 5 recursive generations using nucleus sampling ($p=0.95$, $T=1.0$) as the default decoding strategy. Each generation $k+1$ is produced by prompting the model with the corresponding generation-$k$ text.\n87\t\n88\t\\paragraph{Decoding Strategy Ablation.} Using Qwen2.5-1.5B, we additionally generate chains under four alternative strategies: greedy decoding, nucleus $p=0.9$, $T=0.7$, and $T=1.2$.\n89\t\n90\t\\paragraph{Cross-Scale Probe.} We generate a smaller chain (1,000 documents, depth 0--3) with Qwen2.5-7B to probe scale dependence.\n91\t\n92\t\\paragraph{Retrain Chain.} To validate ecological validity, we construct a retrain chain with Pythia-1.4B: at each generation, the model is fine-tuned via LoRA (rank 16) on the previous generation's output before generating the next.\n93\t\n94\t\\subsection{Feature Extraction}\n95\t\n96\tWe extract a 15-dimensional feature vector for each document, organized into three groups:\n97\t\n98\t\\begin{itemize}\n99\t\\item \\textbf{Vocabulary Diversity} (5 features): type-token ratio, hapax legomena ratio, entropy of unigram distribution, mean log-frequency rank, vocabulary richness index.\n100\t\\item \\textbf{Surprisal Dynamics} (5 features): mean/variance of token-level surprisal, burstiness (autocorrelation of surprisal), perplexity, surprisal skewness.\n101\t\\item \\textbf{Tail Thinning} (5 features): rare token frequency (bottom 10\\%), out-of-top-$k$ mass, type frequency decay exponent, singleton ratio, hapax-dis ratio.\n102\t\\end{itemize}\n103\t\n104\t\\subsection{Classification Methods}\n105\t\n106\t\\paragraph{Pairwise Discrimination.} For each consecutive pair $(k, k+1)$, we train a logistic regression classifier on the 15D features and report AUC and Jensen--Shannon divergence (JSD) between feature distributions.\n107\t\n108\t\\paragraph{Ordinal Depth Estimation.} We compare three approaches: (1) CORAL ordinal regression~\\cite{coral}, which encodes ordinal structure via binary threshold tasks; (2) standard multinomial logistic regression; (3) classical ordinal regression (cumulative logit model). All use the same 15D features with 5-fold cross-validation.\n109\t\n110\t\\paragraph{Baselines.} We compare against: (i) existing binary detectors (DivEye, Binoculars) using confidence scores as depth proxies; (ii) LLM zero-shot depth estimation (GPT-4o, Claude 3.5 Sonnet); (iii) frozen LM embeddings (2048D) with a 2-layer MLP.\n111\t\n112\t\\section{Results}\n113\t\n114\t\\subsection{$K^*=2$: A Robust Empirical Constant}\n115\t\\label{sec:kstar}\n116\t\n117\t\\begin{table}[t]\n118\t\\centering\n119\t\\small\n120\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n121\t\\toprule\n122\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{0v1} & \\textbf{1v2} & \\textbf{2v3} & \\textbf{3v4} & $K^*$ \\\\\n123\t\\midrule\n124\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\emph{AUC (mean $\\pm$ std)}} \\\\\n125\tPythia-1.4B & .993\\tiny{$\\pm$.001} & .672\\tiny{$\\pm$.009} & .576\\tiny{$\\pm$.013} & .538\\tiny{$\\pm$.008} & 2 \\\\\n126\tOLMo-1B & .991\\tiny{$\\pm$.001} & .648\\tiny{$\\pm$.011} & .562\\tiny{$\\pm$.014} & .531\\tiny{$\\pm$.009} & 2 \\\\\n127\tQwen2.5-1.5B & .996\\tiny{$\\pm$.001} & .696\\tiny{$\\pm$.008} & .589\\tiny{$\\pm$.012} & .543\\tiny{$\\pm$.010} & 2 \\\\\n128\t\\midrule\n129\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\emph{JSD}} \\\\\n130\tPythia-1.4B & .241 & .014 & .005 & .003 & -- \\\\\n131\tOLMo-1B & .218 & .012 & .004 & .003 & -- \\\\\n132\tQwen2.5-1.5B & .267 & .016 & .006 & .003 & -- \\\\\n133\t\\bottomrule\n134\t\\end{tabular}\n135\t\\caption{Pairwise discrimination across three model families. All models yield $K^*=2$ at threshold $\\theta=0.60$. JSD decays exponentially from the 0v1 boundary (0.218--0.267) to near-zero by generation 3.}\n136\t\\label{tab:kstar_main}\n137\t\\end{table}\n138\t\n139\t\\Cref{tab:kstar_main} presents the central result. Across all three model families, $K^*=2$: the pairwise AUC between generation 1 and generation 2 ranges from 0.648 to 0.696 (above $\\theta=0.60$), while AUC between generation 2 and generation 3 drops to 0.562--0.589 (below or near $\\theta$). The JSD between consecutive generations decays by an order of magnitude from 0v1 (0.218--0.267) to 1v2 (0.012--0.016), and reaches a floor below 0.005 by generation 3.\n140\t\n141\tThis consistency across architectures (GPT-NeoX, Transformer++, Qwen-style), training data (The Pile, Dolma, multilingual), and model sizes within the 1--2B range constitutes strong evidence that $K^*=2$ is a property of the scale regime rather than any specific model.\n142\t\n143\t\\paragraph{Decoding Strategy Stability.} \\Cref{tab:decoding} shows that $K^*=2$ is stable across four decoding strategies. Greedy decoding produces the largest initial JSD (0.302) because deterministic outputs diverge most from human text, yet $K^*$ remains 2. Only $T=1.2$ shows instability: one of three seeds yields $K^*=3$, as high-temperature sampling preserves more per-generation variance, delaying saturation. We conclude that $K^*=2$ is the default for standard decoding, with extreme temperature as the only identified modulating factor.\n144\t\n145\t\\begin{table}[t]\n146\t\\centering\n147\t\\small\n148\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n149\t\\toprule\n150\t\\textbf{Strategy} & \\textbf{AUC 1v2} & \\textbf{AUC 2v3} & \\textbf{JSD 0v1} & $K^*$ \\\\\n151\t\\midrule\n152\tGreedy & .714 & .603 & .302 & 2 \\\\\n153\tNucleus $p$=0.9 & .688 & .582 & .258 & 2 \\\\\n154\t$T$=0.7 & .708 & .597 & .289 & 2 \\\\\n155\t$T$=1.2 & .651 & .583 & .195 & 2\\textsuperscript{$\\dagger$} \\\\\n156\tBaseline ($p$=0.95) & .696 & .589 & .267 & 2 \\\\\n157\t\\bottomrule\n158\t\\end{tabular}\n159\t\\caption{Decoding strategy ablation (Qwen2.5-1.5B). $^\\dagger$One of three seeds yields $K^*=3$.}\n160\t\\label{tab:decoding}\n161\t\\end{table}\n162\t\n163\t\\paragraph{Base vs.\\ Instruct.} Both Qwen2.5-1.5B base and instruct variants yield $K^*=2$ (AUC 1v2: 0.682 vs.\\ 0.696). The instruct model shows lower vocabulary diversity at gen-1 (0.54 vs.\\ 0.58; KS test $p=0.01$), suggesting that alignment training acts as a mild form of mode collapse, but this does not affect the saturation boundary.\n164\t\n165\t\\paragraph{Cross-Scale Probe.} Qwen2.5-7B yields AUC 2v3 = 0.612 averaged across three seeds, with individual seeds producing $K^*$ values of 2, 3, and 2 (majority: 2). This provides suggestive but inconclusive evidence that $K^*$ may increase with model scale; the effect is within seed variance at our sample size (1K documents). Ordinal accuracy improves to 0.44 (vs.\\ 0.42 for 1.5B), consistent with the larger model's richer channel preserving more distinguishable structure.\n166\t\n167\t\\subsection{Threshold Sensitivity}\n168\t\\label{sec:threshold_sensitivity}\n169\t\n170\t\\begin{table}[t]\n171\t\\centering\n172\t\\small\n173\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n174\t\\toprule\n175\t$\\theta$ & Pythia & OLMo & Qwen & Qwen ($T$=1.2) \\\\\n176\t\\midrule\n177\t0.55 & 3 & 2 & 2 & 3 \\\\\n178\t0.60 & 2 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\\n179\t0.65 & 2 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\\n180\t0.70 & 1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\\n181\t\\bottomrule\n182\t\\end{tabular}\n183\t\\caption{$K^*$ under varying AUC thresholds. $K^*=2$ is stable across the $[0.60, 0.65]$ range for all models.}\n184\t\\label{tab:threshold}\n185\t\\end{table}\n186\t\n187\tTo address potential concerns about threshold arbitrariness, \\Cref{tab:threshold} reports $K^*$ under four threshold values. $K^*=2$ is perfectly stable in the range $\\theta \\in [0.60, 0.65]$. At $\\theta=0.55$ (near chance), some models shift to $K^*=3$; at $\\theta=0.70$ (overly strict), most drop to $K^*=1$. The operating point $\\theta=0.60$ balances between these extremes.\n188\t\n189\t\\subsection{Degradation Ordering}\n190\t\\label{sec:ordering}\n191\t\n192\t\\begin{table}[t]\n193\t\\centering\n194\t\\small\n195\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n196\t\\toprule\n197\t\\textbf{Feature Group} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{Pythia}} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{OLMo}} \\\\\n198\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\n199\t & AUC 1v2 & Sat. & AUC 1v2 & Sat. \\\\\n200\t\\midrule"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018paoR6xr2NTD6NpJCLtAVH","session_id":"23a92ae3-d412-49aa-a360-fb90b813cfb5","uuid":"6e9b6f93-3d05-46bb-8870-65c378fcfcfc","timestamp":"2026-05-12T20:44:28.117Z"}
