{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0162vDX5r3LPFSKp6kXNKuRB","type":"tool_result","content":"200\t\\midrule\n201\tVocab.\\ Diversity & .641 & 1.8 & .625 & 1.6 \\\\\n202\tSurprisal Dynamics & .608 & 2.3 & .612 & 2.1 \\\\\n203\tTail Thinning & .622 & 2.0 & .598 & 2.5 \\\\\n204\t\\bottomrule\n205\t\\end{tabular}\n206\t\\caption{Feature group saturation depths and pairwise AUC (1v2). Vocabulary diversity saturates earliest in both models. ``Sat.'' indicates interpolated saturation depth.}\n207\t\\label{tab:degradation}\n208\t\\end{table}\n209\t\n210\t\\Cref{tab:degradation} presents per-feature-group analysis. Vocabulary diversity saturates earliest across all three model families (depth 1.6--1.8), consistent with the ``rich-get-richer'' amplification of high-frequency tokens under recursive sampling. However, the ordering between surprisal dynamics and tail-thinning features is model-dependent: Pythia shows tail-thinning saturating before surprisal (depth 2.0 vs.\\ 2.3), while OLMo shows the reverse (2.5 vs.\\ 2.1). Cross-model Kendall $\\tau$ for the full ordering is 0.67 ($p=0.08$ for Pythia--OLMo), indicating a tendency rather than a strict universal ordering. OLMo and Qwen show perfect rank agreement ($\\tau=1.0$, $p=0.02$).\n211\t\n212\tWe characterize this finding conservatively: \\emph{vocabulary diversity consistently degrades earliest, while the relative ordering of surprisal and tail features is model-dependent}.\n213\t\n214\t\\subsection{Depth Estimation Methods}\n215\t\n216\t\\begin{table}[t]\n217\t\\centering\n218\t\\small\n219\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n220\t\\toprule\n221\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Acc.} & \\textbf{MAE} & \\textbf{Adj.\\ Acc.} \\\\\n222\t\\midrule\n223\tCORAL (ordinal) & .42 & 1.02 & .74 \\\\\n224\tMultinomial & .40 & 1.18 & .68 \\\\\n225\tOrdinal Regression & .41 & 1.05 & .72 \\\\\n226\t\\bottomrule\n227\t\\end{tabular}\n228\t\\caption{Depth estimation methods on Qwen2.5-1.5B chains. CORAL achieves the lowest MAE and highest adjacent accuracy, demonstrating the value of ordinal structure.}\n229\t\\label{tab:ordinal}\n230\t\\end{table}\n231\t\n232\t\\Cref{tab:ordinal} compares depth estimation approaches. CORAL ordinal regression achieves the best MAE (1.02 vs.\\ 1.18 for multinomial) and adjacent accuracy (0.74 vs.\\ 0.68), though top-1 accuracy differences are modest (0.42 vs.\\ 0.40). The advantage of ordinal modeling lies in error calibration: CORAL's threshold structure penalizes large ordinal errors, producing predictions that are within one generation of the truth 74\\% of the time.\n233\t\n234\t\\paragraph{Hand-Crafted vs.\\ Learned Features.} Frozen Qwen2.5-1.5B embeddings (2048D) with a 2-layer MLP achieve slightly higher accuracy (0.44) and lower MAE (0.95) than our 15D hand-crafted features (0.42, 1.02). The gap is small despite the $136\\times$ dimensionality difference, and hand-crafted features enable the interpretable degradation ordering analysis in \\Cref{sec:ordering}.\n235\t\n236\t\\subsection{Baselines: Existing Methods Fail at Depth Estimation}\n237\t\n238\t\\begin{table}[t]\n239\t\\centering\n240\t\\small\n241\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n242\t\\toprule\n243\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Bin.\\ AUC} & \\textbf{$\\rho$(depth)} & \\textbf{AUC 1v2} & \\textbf{MAE} \\\\\n244\t\\midrule\n245\tDivEye & .93 & $-$.31 & .57 & -- \\\\\n246\tBinoculars & .95 & $-$.28 & .55 & -- \\\\\n247\tGPT-4o (0-shot) & .88 & -- & -- & 1.85 \\\\\n248\tClaude 3.5 (0-shot) & .91 & -- & -- & 1.72 \\\\\n249\tOur 15D + CORAL & .996 & $-$.82 & .70 & 1.02 \\\\\n250\t\\bottomrule\n251\t\\end{tabular}\n252\t\\caption{Baseline comparison. Binary detectors achieve high binary AUC but fail as depth proxies (Spearman $\\rho \\approx -0.3$). LLM zero-shot depth estimation yields high MAE. Our method achieves strong correlation with true depth.}\n253\t\\label{tab:baselines}\n254\t\\end{table}\n255\t\n256\t\\Cref{tab:baselines} demonstrates that existing methods cannot perform depth estimation. Binary detectors (DivEye, Binoculars) achieve excellent binary AUC (0.93--0.95) but their confidence scores correlate weakly with true depth (Spearman $\\rho \\approx -0.3$). Pairwise AUC for gen-1 vs.\\ gen-2 is near chance (0.55--0.57), confirming that binary confidence does not encode depth information.\n257\t\n258\tLLM zero-shot depth estimation also fails: GPT-4o and Claude 3.5 Sonnet achieve 6-class accuracies of only 22\\% and 24\\% respectively (chance: 16.7\\%), with MAE of 1.85 and 1.72. Both models perform well on the binary subtask (gen-0 vs.\\ gen-1 accuracy 81--84\\%) but cannot distinguish deeper generations. This confirms that depth estimation is a non-trivial task requiring specialized methods.\n259\t\n260\t\\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}\n261\t\n262\t\\begin{table}[t]\n263\t\\centering\n264\t\\small\n265\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n266\t\\toprule\n267\t\\textbf{Train$\\backslash$Test} & \\textbf{Pythia} & \\textbf{OLMo} & \\textbf{Qwen} \\\\\n268\t\\midrule\n269\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\emph{Binary AUC (retention: 92\\%)}} \\\\\n270\tPythia & .993 & .91 & .89 \\\\\n271\tOLMo & .90 & .991 & .88 \\\\\n272\tQwen & .92 & .90 & .996 \\\\\n273\t\\midrule\n274\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\emph{Ordinal Accuracy (retention: 58\\%)}} \\\\\n275\tPythia & .40 & .26 & .24 \\\\\n276\tOLMo & .25 & .39 & .23 \\\\\n277\tQwen & .28 & .26 & .42 \\\\\n278\t\\bottomrule\n279\t\\end{tabular}\n280\t\\caption{Cross-model transfer. Binary detection transfers well (92\\% retention). Ordinal depth estimation transfers poorly (58\\%), indicating model-specific depth signatures.}\n281\t\\label{tab:transfer}\n282\t\\end{table}\n283\t\n284\t\\Cref{tab:transfer} reveals an asymmetry: binary detection features transfer well across model families (AUC 0.88--0.92, 92\\% retention of in-distribution performance), but ordinal depth estimation transfers poorly (accuracy 0.23--0.28, 58\\% retention). This indicates that the human/synthetic boundary involves universal features shared across LM families, while the finer-grained depth signal is model-specific---different architectures produce different ``fingerprints'' of mode collapse at each generation. This constrains the practical deployment of graduated filtering to settings where the generating model family is approximately known.\n285\t\n286\t\\subsection{Graduated Filtering}\n287\t\\label{sec:filtering}\n288\t\n289\t\\begin{table}[t]\n290\t\\centering\n291\t\\small\n292\t\\begin{tabular}{lccl}\n293\t\\toprule\n294\t\\textbf{Strategy} & \\textbf{MMLU} & \\textbf{HSwag} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ vs Binary} \\\\\n295\t\\midrule\n296\tNo filter & 25.8\\tiny{$\\pm$0.3} & 42.1\\tiny{$\\pm$0.4} & $-$0.6 / $-$0.9 \\\\\n297\tBinary & 26.4\\tiny{$\\pm$0.4} & 43.0\\tiny{$\\pm$0.5} & --- \\\\\n298\tSurprisal-based & 26.9\\tiny{$\\pm$0.3} & 43.4\\tiny{$\\pm$0.4} & +0.5 / +0.4 \\\\\n299\tGraduated (3-class) & 27.2\\tiny{$\\pm$0.4} & 43.7\\tiny{$\\pm$0.5} & +0.8 / +0.7 \\\\\n300\t\\bottomrule\n301\t\\end{tabular}\n302\t\\caption{Downstream evaluation after continued pretraining with 30\\% synthetic contamination. Graduated filtering outperforms binary by 0.8 pp (MMLU) and 0.7 pp (HellaSwag). Standard deviations are across 3 seeds.}\n303\t\\label{tab:downstream}\n304\t\\end{table}\n305\t\n306\t\\Cref{tab:downstream} presents downstream results at the 30\\% contamination level. Graduated filtering (three-class: human/gen-1/gen-2+) outperforms binary filtering by 0.8 pp on MMLU and 0.7 pp on HellaSwag. While the absolute gains are modest and the confidence intervals may overlap given seed variance ($\\sigma \\approx 0.4$--0.5 pp), the consistent direction across both benchmarks and the mechanistic explanation support a real effect.\n307\t\n308\tThe mechanism is \\emph{calibration near the decision boundary}: binary filtering labels all synthetic text identically, discarding usable gen-1 text alongside degraded gen-3+ text. Graduated filtering assigns gen-1 a ``shallow synthetic'' label, allowing partial retention of higher-quality synthetic data while aggressively filtering deeply recursive text.\n309\t\n310\t\\paragraph{Dose-Response.} \\Cref{tab:dose} shows that the graduated filtering advantage scales with contamination severity: from +0.2 pp at 10\\% injection to +1.1 pp at 50\\%. This follows from the fact that at higher contamination rates, gen-1 text constitutes a larger absolute fraction of the corpus, amplifying the value of calibrated retention decisions.\n311\t\n312\t\\begin{table}[t]\n313\t\\centering\n314\t\\small\n315\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n316\t\\toprule\n317\t\\textbf{Injection} & \\textbf{No Filt.} & \\textbf{Binary} & \\textbf{Grad.} & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n318\t\\midrule\n319\t10\\% & 26.1 & 26.3 & 26.5 & +0.2 \\\\\n320\t30\\% & 25.8 & 26.4 & 27.2 & +0.8 \\\\\n321\t50\\% & 25.2 & 26.2 & 27.3 & +1.1 \\\\\n322\t\\bottomrule\n323\t\\end{tabular}\n324\t\\caption{MMLU accuracy under varying contamination rates. Graduated filtering advantage grows super-linearly with contamination severity.}\n325\t\\label{tab:dose}\n326\t\\end{table}\n327\t\n328\t\\subsection{Ecological Validity}\n329\t\n330\t\\paragraph{Retrain Chain.} A LoRA retrain chain with Pythia-1.4B yields $K^*=2$, with AUC values slightly lower than the prompt chain (1v2: 0.635 vs.\\ 0.672; $\\Delta = -0.037$). Feature distributions show significant but moderate shifts (vocabulary diversity KS test $p=0.03$), confirming that the saturation boundary is qualitatively robust to the recursion mechanism.\n331\t\n332\t\\paragraph{Common Crawl Validation.} Applying our classifier to a Common Crawl 2025--2026 sample, we predict 72\\% human, 18\\% gen-1, and 10\\% gen-2+ text. Predicted depth correlates with vocabulary richness ($r=0.41$) and AlignScore ($r=0.34$). Manual inspection of predicted gen-2+ samples shows visible quality degradation in 70\\% of cases. These correlations are modest due to domain shift, but directionally consistent with our controlled findings.\n333\t\n334\t\\section{Discussion}\n335\t\n336\t\\paragraph{$K^*$ as a Universal Constant.} The stability of $K^*=2$ across three architectures, four decoding strategies, base/instruct variants, and prompt/retrain chains is striking. We interpret this as reflecting a fundamental property of the 1--2B scale regime: the lossy channel defined by models of this size exhausts distinguishable generational information by the second recursive step. The suggestive evidence from the 7B probe ($K^*$ occasionally reaching 3) aligns with the expectation that larger channel capacity delays saturation, though confirming this requires larger-scale experiments.\n337\t\n338\t\\paragraph{Practical Implications.} For data curators, $K^*=2$ implies that the actionable depth signal is coarse---three classes suffice. This simplifies deployment: a lightweight 15D feature extractor with a CORAL classifier can provide depth-aware quality scores. The cross-model transfer results (\\Cref{tab:transfer}) indicate that model-family-specific training is needed for accurate depth estimation, but binary detection generalizes well.\n339\t\n340\t\\paragraph{Limitations.} Our experiments use 1--2B parameter models; $K^*$ at the 70B+ scale remains unmeasured. The downstream evaluation uses continued pretraining of small models, which may not reflect the dynamics of large-scale pretraining from scratch. The 0.8 pp improvement from graduated filtering, while consistent, may not reach statistical significance at $p<0.05$ with three seeds. The retrain chain uses LoRA rather than full fine-tuning, which may underestimate parameter-level collapse effects.\n341\t\n342\t\\section{Conclusion}\n343\t\n344\tWe introduced generational depth estimation as a prediction task and the saturation boundary $K^*$ as a measurable quantity characterizing recursive synthetic text. Through systematic experiments, we established $K^*=2$ as a robust empirical constant for 1--2B parameter language models---stable across three model families, four decoding strategies, and base/instruct variants. Vocabulary diversity degrades earliest in the recursive chain, though the full degradation ordering is partially model-dependent. Even the coarse three-class signal enabled by $K^*=2$ provides better-calibrated data curation than binary filtering, with growing advantages as web data contamination intensifies. Our work provides both a theoretical framework (DPI-derived saturation) and practical tools (15D features + ordinal classifier) for depth-aware data governance.\n345\t\n346\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n347\t\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n348\t\n349\t\\bibitem[Shumailov et~al.(2024)]{shumailov2024ai}\n350\tIlia Shumailov, Zakhar Shumaylov, Yiren Zhao, Yarin Gal, Nicolas Papernot, and Ross Anderson.\n351\t\\newblock {AI} models collapse when trained on recursively generated data.\n352\t\\newblock \\emph{Nature}, 631:755--759, 2024.\n353\t\n354\t\\bibitem[Mitchell et~al.(2023)]{minkpp}\n355\tEric Mitchell, Yoonho Lee, Alexander Khazatsky, Christopher~D. Manning, and Chelsea Finn.\n356\t\\newblock Detectgpt: Zero-shot machine-generated text detection using probability curvature.\n357\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ICML}, 2023.\n358\t\n359\t\\bibitem[Hans et~al.(2024)]{binoculars}\n360\tAbhimanyu Hans, Avi Schwarzschild, Valeriia Cherepanova, Hamid Kazemi, Aniruddha Saha, Micah Goldblum, Jonas Geiping, and Tom Goldstein.\n361\t\\newblock Spotting {LLM}s with binoculars: Zero-shot detection of machine-generated text.\n362\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ICML}, 2024.\n363\t\n364\t\\bibitem[Tulchinskii et~al.(2024)]{diveye}\n365\tEduard Tulchinskii, Kristian Kuznetsov, Laida Kushnareva, Daniil Cherniavskii, Sergey Nikolenko, Evgeny Burnaev, Serguei Barannikov, and Irina Piontkovskaya.\n366\t\\newblock Intrinsic dimension estimation for robust detection of {AI}-generated texts.\n367\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of NeurIPS}, 2024.\n368\t\n369\t\\bibitem[Cao et~al.(2020)]{coral}\n370\tWenzhi Cao, Vahid Mirjalili, and Sebastian Raschka.\n371\t\\newblock Rank consistent ordinal regression for neural networks with application to age estimation.\n372\t\\newblock \\emph{Pattern Recognition Letters}, 140:325--331, 2020.\n373\t\n374\t\\bibitem[Feng et~al.(2024)]{feng2024}\n375\tShangbin Feng, Vidhisha Balachandran, Yuyang Bai, and Yulia Tsvetkov.\n376\t\\newblock Fact-checking the output of large language models via token-level uncertainty quantification.\n377\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ACL}, 2024.\n378\t\n379\t\\bibitem[Yi et~al.(2024)]{yi2024}\n380\tJiayi Yi, Zirui Liu, and Xiangliang Zhang.\n381\t\\newblock On the reliability of large language models to misinformed and underinformed users.\n382\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of NAACL}, 2024.\n383\t\n384\t\\end{thebibliography}\n385\t\n386\t\\appendix\n387\t\n388\t\\section{Detailed Retrain-Chain Results}\n389\t\\label{app:retrain}\n390\t\n391\t\\begin{table}[h]\n392\t\\centering\n393\t\\small\n394\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n395\t\\toprule\n396\t\\textbf{Pair} & \\textbf{Prompt AUC} & \\textbf{Retrain AUC} & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n397\t\\midrule\n398\t0 vs 1 & .993 & .988 & $-$.005 \\\\\n399\t1 vs 2 & .672 & .635 & $-$.037 \\\\\n400\t2 vs 3 & .576 & .572 & $-$.004 \\\\\n401\t3 vs 4 & .538 & .541 & +.003 \\\\\n402\t\\bottomrule\n403\t\\end{tabular}\n404\t\\caption{Prompt-chain vs.\\ retrain-chain (Pythia-1.4B). The largest difference is at 1v2 ($\\Delta=-0.037$); $K^*=2$ in both conditions.}\n405\t\\end{table}\n406\t\n407\t\\section{LLM Zero-Shot Per-Pair Accuracy}\n408\t\\label{app:llm}\n409\t\n410\t\\begin{table}[h]\n411\t\\centering\n412\t\\small\n413\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n414\t\\toprule\n415\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{0v1 Acc} & \\textbf{1v2 Acc} & \\textbf{2v3 Acc} \\\\\n416\t\\midrule\n417\tGPT-4o & .81 & .39 & .28 \\\\\n418\tClaude 3.5 Sonnet & .84 & .42 & .30 \\\\\n419\tOur CORAL & .99+ & .68 & -- \\\\\n420\t\\bottomrule\n421\t\\end{tabular}\n422\t\\caption{Per-pair accuracy for LLM zero-shot depth estimation. Performance degrades sharply beyond the 0v1 boundary.}\n423\t\\end{table}\n424\t\n425\t\\end{document}\n426\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018paoR6xr2NTD6NpJCLtAVH","session_id":"23a92ae3-d412-49aa-a360-fb90b813cfb5","uuid":"bb38223e-253b-401a-93ed-e3c5a5d64512","timestamp":"2026-05-12T20:44:34.555Z"}
